            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>A Campaign That Started but Failed to Scale</h2>
<p>A utility app planned to launch a new set of creatives and increase its daily budget by roughly 20%. The media team created the campaigns in the morning and launched the learning process. The account appeared to have sufficient balance, and no payment error had been reported. After the budget was adjusted at noon, the platform began rejecting some budget updates, while several existing campaigns gradually lost spending momentum.</p>
<p>The media team initially suspected narrow targeting or weak bids. A review of payment records revealed the actual issue: the account’s available balance was nearly depleted. This was not simply a case of forgetting to top up. Although balance alerts had been enabled, they relied on manual checks, and the thresholds, balance definitions, and ownership of follow-up actions were unclear. The alerts therefore did not trigger timely action, and the top-up was completed after the scaling window had begun.</p>
<h2>Step 1: Identify What “Insufficient Balance” Actually Means</h2>
<p>Prepayment, settlement, and approval processes differ across advertising platforms. Meta may restrict delivery because of payment method issues, account review, or failed payments. Google Ads balances may be affected by automatic payments, payment records, and budget changes. TikTok Ads often involves differences among account balance, credit, and top-up processing time.</p>
<p>Instead of applying one universal low-balance threshold, teams should track three figures:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Book balance:</strong> Funds shown in the platform but not yet available for settlement;</li>
<li><strong>Spendable balance:</strong> Funds that can currently participate in bidding;</li>
<li><strong>Recent average daily spend:</strong> Average expenditure over the previous three to seven days.</li>
</ul>
<p>Book balance alone may hide restricted credit, chargebacks, or failed payments. Recent daily spend alone may underestimate funding needs during promotions or budget increases. A more reliable approach is to estimate how many days the available funds can cover based on the expected consumption rate.</p>
<h2>Step 2: Replace Simple Alerts with Three Escalation Levels</h2>
<p>Use different risk levels instead of the same threshold for every account:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Alert level</th>
<th>Trigger</th>
<th>Required action</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Yellow</td>
<td>Available funds cover less than five days at the recent spending rate</td>
<td>Verify the payment method, top-up channel, and expected processing time</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Orange</td>
<td>Funds may run out within 48–72 hours under the current plan</td>
<td>Start a top-up and prepare a backup payment method</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Red</td>
<td>Balance is critically low, charges are restricted, or the top-up failed</td>
<td>Pause new budget increases and restore existing planned delivery first</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Thresholds should change with spending plans. Accounts launching new campaigns, scaling creatives, or entering a major promotion need a larger safety margin. Low-spend accounts should not use the same funding standard as high-budget accounts.</p>
<p>In a multi-account environment, teams can review balances across platforms and flag accounts according to their consumption rate. The key is not another notification number, but a clear owner and response deadline attached to every alert.</p>
<h2>Step 3: A Successful Top-Up Submission Is Not the Same as Available Funds</h2>
<p>Once a balance risk is detected, the team must verify that the funds have actually arrived. A simple funding log should record the account ID, platform, currency, amount, submission time, expected processing time, actual credit time, and responsible owner.</p>
<p>For top-ups requiring manual review or cross-border settlement, a successful submission does not mean the balance is available. Major budget increases should not be made until the spendable balance and payment status are confirmed. A backup payment method can also reduce disruption caused by card limits, verification failures, or bank review delays.</p>
<p>A safer sequence is to verify the account status, test the payment process with a small amount, restore existing campaigns, and then increase budgets step by step. This avoids disrupting the platform’s learning process through a sudden budget change.</p>
<h2>Step 4: Measure How Early the Risk Was Detected</h2>
<p>A post-incident review should not focus only on how much delivery was lost. It should answer several operational questions: How early did the system identify the risk? Who confirmed that the top-up had arrived? Was the backup payment method ready? Did budget increases align with funding status?</p>
<p>After revising its process, the utility app linked balance monitoring with daily spend, budget changes, and payment errors. It introduced yellow, orange, and red alert levels for each account. No budgets were increased until funds were confirmed as available. The team restored planned delivery first and then scaled gradually.</p>
<p>This change cannot prevent every payment issue, but it shifts the response from reacting after delivery stops to completing the necessary action before an interruption occurs. For companies managing overseas advertising accounts and multiple ad platforms, effective balance alerts create a closed loop among budget changes, financial top-ups, and media delivery—reducing the risk that funding problems interrupt customer acquisition plans.</p>
